WEBVTT

00:00.140 --> 00:02.570
- [Instructor] Let's say you
wanted to solve this equation,

00:02.570 --> 00:05.666
two to the x squared minus three power

00:05.666 --> 00:10.666
is equal to one over the cube root of x.

00:10.680 --> 00:13.823
Pause the video and see
if you can solve this.

00:13.823 --> 00:17.833
Well you probably realize that
this is not so easy to solve.

00:17.833 --> 00:20.331
The way that I would at least
attempt to tackle it is,

00:20.331 --> 00:23.960
you would say this is two
to the x squared minus three

00:23.960 --> 00:26.990
is equal to x to the,

00:26.990 --> 00:29.620
I could rewrite this, this
is one over x to the 1/3,

00:29.620 --> 00:33.030
so this is x to the negative 1/3 power.

00:33.030 --> 00:35.413
Maybe I can simplify it
by raising both sides

00:35.413 --> 00:37.803
to the negative three power.

00:37.803 --> 00:39.206
And so then I would get,

00:39.206 --> 00:40.453
if I raise something to an exponent,

00:40.453 --> 00:41.990
then raise that to an exponent,

00:41.990 --> 00:43.460
I could just multiply the exponents.

00:43.460 --> 00:46.473
So it would be two to the
negative three x squared

00:46.473 --> 00:48.190
plus nine power.

00:48.190 --> 00:51.710
I just multiplied both of these
terms times negative three,

00:51.710 --> 00:55.260
is equal to x to the negative
1/3 to the negative three.

00:55.260 --> 00:57.850
Negative 1/3 times
negative three is just 1,

00:57.850 --> 00:59.582
so that's just going to be equal to x.

00:59.582 --> 01:04.120
So it looks a little bit
simpler, but still not so easy.

01:04.120 --> 01:06.460
I could try to take log
base two of both sides

01:06.460 --> 01:09.587
and I'd get negative
three x squared plus nine

01:09.587 --> 01:13.190
is equal to log base two of x.

01:13.190 --> 01:16.650
But once again not havin'
an easy time solve this.

01:16.650 --> 01:18.660
And the reason why I
gave you this equation

01:18.660 --> 01:20.439
is to appreciate that some equations

01:20.439 --> 01:22.807
are not so easy to solve algebraically.

01:22.807 --> 01:25.995
But we have other tools, we
have things like computers,

01:25.995 --> 01:28.083
we can graph things and they can

01:28.083 --> 01:29.450
at least get us really close

01:29.450 --> 01:32.370
to knowing what the solution is.

01:32.370 --> 01:33.564
And the way that we can do that

01:33.564 --> 01:37.156
is we can say hey, well
what if I had one function

01:37.156 --> 01:42.156
or one equation, that
was y is equal to two x,

01:42.260 --> 01:45.210
two to the x squared
minus three I should say.

01:45.210 --> 01:47.697
And you had another that was y is equal

01:47.697 --> 01:52.697
to one over the cube root of x.

01:52.790 --> 01:55.280
And then you could graph each of these

01:55.280 --> 01:57.260
and then you could see
where they intersect.

01:57.260 --> 01:58.581
Because where they intersect

01:58.581 --> 02:01.266
that means two to the
x squared minus three

02:01.266 --> 02:05.170
is giving you the same y as
one over the cube root of x.

02:05.170 --> 02:06.550
Or another way to think
about it is they're going

02:06.550 --> 02:08.470
to intersect at an x value,

02:08.470 --> 02:13.470
where these two expressions
are equal to each other.

02:13.950 --> 02:15.660
And so what we could do is we could go

02:15.660 --> 02:17.651
to a graphing calculator,
or we could go to a site

02:17.651 --> 02:19.960
like Desmos and graph it and at least try

02:19.960 --> 02:22.890
to approximate what the
point of intersection is,

02:22.890 --> 02:24.500
and so let's do that.

02:24.500 --> 02:27.603
So I graphed this ahead of time on Desmos.

02:28.520 --> 02:32.900
So you can see here, this is
our two sides of our equation

02:32.900 --> 02:35.390
but now we've expressed
each of them as a function.

02:35.390 --> 02:39.920
Right here in blue we
have two, we have f of x,

02:39.920 --> 02:43.358
or I could even say this is
y is equal to f of x which

02:43.358 --> 02:47.170
is equal to two to the
x squared minus three.

02:47.170 --> 02:50.620
And then in this yellowish color I have y

02:50.620 --> 02:52.770
is equal to g of x which is equal

02:52.770 --> 02:56.090
to one over the cube root of x,

02:56.090 --> 02:58.410
and we can see where they intersect.

02:58.410 --> 03:00.417
They intersect right over there.

03:00.417 --> 03:02.490
And we're not going to
get an exact answer,

03:02.490 --> 03:04.030
but even at this level of zoom

03:04.030 --> 03:05.836
and on a tool like Desmos
you can keep zooming in

03:05.836 --> 03:08.320
in order to get to get a
more and more precise answer.

03:08.320 --> 03:09.416
In fact you can even scroll over this

03:09.416 --> 03:11.780
and it'll even tell you
where they intersect.

03:11.780 --> 03:12.614
But even if we're trying to approximate,

03:12.614 --> 03:14.860
just looking at the graph.

03:14.860 --> 03:19.590
We can see that the x
value, right over here

03:19.590 --> 03:22.800
it looks like it is happening at around,

03:22.800 --> 03:26.730
let's see this is 1.5, and
each of these is a tenth.

03:26.730 --> 03:29.480
So this is 1.6 and then it looks like

03:29.480 --> 03:32.270
it's about two thirds of
the way to the next one.

03:32.270 --> 03:33.420
So this looks like it's about,

03:33.420 --> 03:36.833
I'll say this is approximately 1.66.

03:39.110 --> 03:41.790
And if you were to actually
find the exact solution,

03:41.790 --> 03:46.320
you would actually find
this awfully close to 1.66.

03:46.320 --> 03:48.057
So the whole point here is, is that,

03:48.057 --> 03:50.830
even when it's algebraically difficult

03:50.830 --> 03:52.570
to solve something you could set up

03:52.570 --> 03:55.891
or restate your problem
or reframe your problem

03:55.891 --> 03:58.940
in a way that makes it easier to solve.

03:58.940 --> 04:02.360
You can set this up as, hey
let's make two functions,

04:02.360 --> 04:05.030
and then let's graph them
and see where they intersect

04:05.030 --> 04:06.520
And the x value where they intersect well

04:06.520 --> 04:08.530
that would be a solution to that equation,

04:08.530 --> 04:10.660
and that's exactly what
we did right there.

04:10.660 --> 04:11.748
We're saying that hey, the x value,

04:11.748 --> 04:16.333
the x solution here is roughly 1.66.
